<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ur was an ancient Sumerian city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, near the former course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It reached its height as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and his successors, who built its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>. Its sack by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> around 2004 BCE ended Ur III, and the city remained a religious centre in later Babylonian times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur.jpg" alt="Ur" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Harris · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urimki_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
